## Definition

Assume the finite-mode light–matter assignment and choose one of its modes. Let $\rho$ be a density operator (a positive trace-class operator of trace one) on that mode's one-mode Fock space. Let $\Pi_n:=|n\rangle\langle n|$ be the ideal number-count PVM on that mode, with probabilities $p_n:=\operatorname{tr}(\rho\Pi_n)$. Suppose $\bar n:=\sum_{n\ge0}np_n$ is finite and strictly positive, and $m_2:=\sum_{n\ge0}n(n-1)p_n$ is finite. Define the model's equal-time second factorial statistic by $g^{(2)}(0;\rho):=m_2/\bar n^2$. This is the number-distribution form of the ideal single-mode normally ordered factorial count statistic; the unambiguous numerator used here is the finite sum $m_2$ defined above. This definition uses the ideal counting outcome model only; it asserts no time-resolved photocurrent law, empirical detection, or detector realization.

